# Accepted privacy findings.
#
# Commit SHAs whose history findings have been reviewed and consciously accepted.
# Only SHAs go here - never the value itself, which would reintroduce the leak into
# the very file that documents it.
#
# The check still reports these; it just stops treating them as fatal. Accepting a
# finding is a decision that should stay visible, not one that makes the evidence
# disappear.

# 6d93f37 - commit message used the real season invoice total as an example.
# Accepted 2026-09-04 by Wesley: the Sharks publish per-section average prices in
# their public season-ticket marketing, so the figure is derivable from public
# information and reveals little. Rewriting published history was judged not worth
# it for this. The commit-msg hook now prevents recurrence.
6d93f37

# c5fa7a3 - a self-test fixture in scripts/check_privacy.py used the real season
# invoice total as its example value, and was pushed to the public repo.
# Accepted 2026-09-05 by Wesley on the same reasoning as 6d93f37: one labelled
# four-digit figure, no seat or account data alongside it, and derivable from the
# Sharks' own public per-section marketing prices. Rewriting 17 commits of published
# history was judged not worth it - and force-pushing would not have removed the
# object anyway, since GitHub keeps it fetchable by SHA until it garbage-collects.
#
# Prevented from recurring by .githooks/pre-commit, which now rejects private values
# in staged CONTENT (the commit-msg hook only ever covered messages). The fixture
# value is now 11111111, with a comment: plausible means real.
c5fa7a3

# 232adf9, dc02a9d - a seat locator for our own seats reached the public repo through a
# scoped comp store and two self-test fixtures, and stayed in the tree from those commits
# until it was keyed out (ops#189). The tree side is fixed; these SHAs record that the
# history side was reviewed and accepted rather than rewritten.
#
# Accepted 2026-09-17 by Wesley, on the ops#169 reasoning that a rewrite reduces
# discoverability and not availability: refs/pull/<n>/head survives a history rewrite on
# GitHub, as do forks and caches, so the operation is both expensive and incomplete. main
# additionally rejects force-pushes with enforce_admins on, so it would have needed
# deliberate unprotection of the one control that makes the rest of rule 5 meaningful.
#
# SCOPE OF THE SET, stated because the comment above claims a review.
# These two are the commits that INTRODUCE the value and are reachable from a remote
# branch. Two others introduce it and are NOT: they are pre-squash commits on branches
# that no remote head contains, so neither CI nor a fresh clone can reach them, and their
# SHAs would resolve to nothing for every reader but a local one. They are described here
# rather than listed, because CLAUDE.md is explicit that a pointer to nothing is worse
# than no pointer - it reads as provenance.
# A third candidate was dropped on review: its tree carries the value but it never changed
# the occurrence count, and the history pass pickaxes with `git log -S` before verifying
# the tree, so it would never have been surfaced to accept in the first place.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING A CLEAN RUN OVER THESE SHAS. Unlike every other entry in this
# file, these are INERT today: the passes never reported them, so there is nothing here
# to stop being fatal. The literal pass cannot carry the pattern, because the same values
# appear legitimately in the two whole-arena stores and the build would fail on data that
# is fine. The structural pass keys on field NAMES and these were not on its list. So the
# entry is a record, not a suppression - and it becomes a real one the day a pattern for
# this class exists.
#
# Recurrence is prevented structurally instead: resale_store.py now refuses the locator
# fields by name, and the property that actually distinguishes a leak here is structural
# rather than literal - a scoped store carrying exactly one of something the whole-arena
# stores carry many of. That check is filed (ops#193), not built.
232adf9
dc02a9d
